From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:33:03 +0200
Subject: phy: intel: grx500-pcie: report a reset that does not complete

The PHY reset is asserted and released once at probe and again on every
init, and both return values were dropped on the floor. The reset
controller polls a status bit for each transition and gives up after a
timeout, so a discarded error here means the line's state is unknown and
the driver carries on as though it had reset the PHY.

Neither caller can do anything useful about it. The PHY has kept working
even when the poll timed out, and failing probe over it would take the
root complex with it. Warn instead, so the condition is visible in the
log rather than silent.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c b/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c
index 79ccdedd083f..5e31415fcb71 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c
@@ -210,8 +210,25 @@ static void intel_pciephy_ssc_disable(struct intel_pciephy *priv)
 
 static void intel_pciephy_reset(struct intel_pciephy *priv)
 {
-	reset_control_assert(priv->rst);
-	reset_control_deassert(priv->rst);
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Neither caller can do anything about a reset that will not complete,
+	 * and the PHY has kept working in practice even when the poll timed
+	 * out. But the reset controller polls a status bit the device tree
+	 * names, so a timeout here means that bit is the wrong one and the
+	 * line's real state is unknown. Say so instead of discarding it.
+	 */
+	ret = reset_control_assert(priv->rst);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(priv->dev, "failed to assert the PHY reset: %d\n",
+			 ret);
+
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(priv->rst);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(priv->dev, "failed to release the PHY reset: %d\n",
+			 ret);
+
 	udelay(1);
 }
 
